#{[#edb/ref 17592186049008 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101683} :activity/id #uuid "ba6b04cd-0e14-5cbc-8d10-bae9558ae0bb" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101961 :tutorial/content "Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3,$ we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 23\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A \\cdot I_3 = A.$ You can check that $I_3 \\cdot A = A$ using the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general: \n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100696 :question/id #uuid "f7868f7f-b296-5a38-9af6-61dc4d1198a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4408" :question/problem "Calculate the product $I_3 B$ given that $I_3$ is the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix and\n![](images/9b/9b8d8f439845a4949789fe313997d2245249e1a4df58c750d7cda0af36f1ce18.png)" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $I_3$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n![](images/ac/ac591cf90fae6e8275fed52c26c8487bff9d654733d414974631948fe30b8150.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101962 :tutorial/content "Whenever we multiply a matrix $A$ by the $n$th column of the identity matrix, the result is the $n$th column of $A.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe first column of the identity matrix $I_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIf we multiply $A$ by the column on the *right*, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product is just the first column of the matrix $A.$\n\nUsing the same method, you can check for yourself that when we multiply $A$ by the second column of $I_2,$ the result is the second column of $A.$\n\nOn the other hand, if we multiply $A$ by the first *row* of the identity matrix $I_2$ on the *left*, we will obtain the first row of $A$. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column of the Identity Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101686}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100697 :question/id #uuid "9832238f-baff-5527-a11a-c65b6529cb0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4410" :question/problem "Calculate the product $C D,$ if\n![](images/94/945664c408577aac1d1a745120b3c17c46fd9db93e6d26cc53a4e501390936e2.png)\nand \n![](images/90/907ffa0b29d96249fe03a7fce3fe682c08982b14c7b7f72840540e6269196425.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix $I_3,$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $C.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/98/980ae75452824b4a8aa701b4e11be8c31448557933357458329f0a2b73a1cca6.png)\n\nDon't believe it? Let's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ -1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column or Row of the Identity Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101965 :tutorial/content "We've been using the fact that $AI_n = I_n A = A$ for any $n \\times n$ matrix $A.$ But how do we know for sure?\n\nLet's prove the case of $n=2.$ That is, let's prove that $AI_2=I_2A = A,$ where $A$ is any $2\\times2$ matrix.\n\nAny general $2 \\times 2$ matrix can be written as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ \n\nThen, multiplying by $I_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11}\\cdot 1+{a}_{12}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{11}\\cdot 0+{a}_{12}\\cdot 1 \\\\ {a}_{21}\\cdot 1+{a}_{22}\\cdot 0 & {a}_{21}\\cdot 0+{a}_{22}\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above proof shows that the property $A I_2 = I_2 A = A$ holds for all $2\\times2$ matrices $A.$\n\nIn general, to prove that the property $AI_n = I_n A = A$ holds for all $n \\times n$ matrices $A,$ we can use the same method." :tutorial/title "Proving Properties Related to the Identity Matrix"}}] :activity/title "Multiplying a Matrix by the Identity Matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101038}] [#edb/ref 17592186050122 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116097} :activity/id #uuid "f5bf1856-47b4-5455-be99-90d735981501" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116486 :tutorial/content "We have already seen some properties of matrix multiplication. For example, multiplying any $n \\times n$ square matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_n$ will leave it unchanged:\n\n$$\nA I_n = I_n A = A.\n$$\n\nAlso, matrix multiplication follows the **distributive laws**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)C & =AC+BC, \\\\ C(A+B) & =CA+CB.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116098}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115056 :question/id #uuid "32bd6b27-e95d-5de4-941f-309eb0bc61f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4411" :question/problem "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n![](images/b4/b4b5ccc4e5075ba81dc840743f0267ba2e5f28d75a5be09de54df111203b949a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-C & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-CB & =(A-C)B \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-B \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Distributive Law"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116487 :tutorial/content "If $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are three matrices that are conformable for multiplication, then they follow the **associative law**:\n\n$$\n\\left( AB\\right) C = A\\left( BC\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, when we wish to compute a product $ABC,$ we don't always have to start by multiplying $AB.$ Alternatively, we can start by multiplying $BC,$ and then multiply $A$ by the result. \n\nSometimes rearranging the parentheses might make the computations slightly easier." :tutorial/title "The Associative Law for Matrix Multiplication"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115057 :question/id #uuid "3b1395b6-49c1-5e0d-ac94-c2d6fc242f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4412" :question/problem "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n![](images/32/32c0a3389207452c52376ce4305109e1c5ed634a07d2c99771825c39b5482940.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115058 :question/id #uuid "7ab158e6-4222-5cab-88a0-85ce74a7d4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4413" :question/problem "Given that $A, B,$ and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n\nII. $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n\nIII. $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\nAB + AC  \\neq BA + CA.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116492 :tutorial/content "In general, matrix multiplication is **not** commutative. In other words, given two general matrices $A$ and $B$ that are conformable for multiplication, we have\n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA.\n$$\n\nHowever, some particular matrices may satisfy this property. For instance, $AI = IA$ (where $I$ is the identity matrix)." :tutorial/title "Matrix Multiplication Is Not Commutative in General"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116101}}] :activity/title "Properties of Matrix Multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115470}] [#edb/ref 17592186050446 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116102} :activity/id #uuid "18afd5ba-844e-5a0a-b7f3-a142f4f4628b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116488 :tutorial/content "Powers of matrices are similar to powers of numbers. If $A$ is a matrix, then $A^n$ is $A$ multiplied by itself $n$ times: \n\n$$\nA^n = \\underbrace{A\\:\\cdot\\: A\\:\\cdot\\: \\ldots \\:\\cdot\\: A}_{\\Large{n\\text{ times}}}\n$$\n\nFor $A$ to be conformable with itself, $A$ needs to be a square matrix. So we can only take powers of square matrices.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that\n![](images/81/81537bed0eafc982114b89fc84baeac44f7a75997c89df5a0f89b8b0a00b2da3.svg)\n\nThen we can calculate $A^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 & 3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 8 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that $A^0 = I,$ the identity matrix of the same dimension as $A.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115059 :question/id #uuid "f832e671-b4bd-5d1d-81e7-b215a8ee4e27" :question/math-academy-id "e-4414" :question/problem "Find $A^4$ given that ![](images/e0/e0e7d5ed9b893352d68931ff5d8fa93f0d0c5173e5955a3f5cdc0cb65d3c3c78.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, to find $A^2,$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A^4 = A^2 \\cdot A^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}81 & 0 \\\\ 100 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116489 :tutorial/content "Now that we know how to compute powers of matrices, we can evaluate polynomials where the input is a square matrix. \n\nFor example, consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3.\n$$\n\nNote that, in order to evaluate this polynomial for an input matrix $X,$ we need to interpret any constants as multiples of the identity matrix. So, we interpret the polynomial as\n\n$$\nf(X) = X^2 + 4X + 3I.\n$$\n\nFor example, if ![](images/6a/6abb7cbdef9c5d7b23d8519468b174de369cfe4af4b9f8c4578ce7dc74812f63.svg) then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can evaluate $f(A)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & ={A}^{2}+4A+3I \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+4+3 & 3+4+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 & 4+8+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Matrix Polynomials"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115060 :question/id #uuid "bea8819f-6feb-5de6-a585-4379378ccdbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4417" :question/problem "Consider the matrix\n![](images/f5/f5b9dd39e03e7a8c984d6a38db8dc32326669d134ff0b5aa012c421308b48351.svg)\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2 \\\\  & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)=-D^2+3D+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate $D^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & =-{D}^{2}+3D+2I \\\\  & =-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3+2 & 10+15+0 \\\\ 0 & -9-9+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 25 \\\\ 0 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial"}}] :activity/title "Powers of Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115471}] [#edb/ref 17592186050021 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847} :activity/id #uuid "f83b8c51-727f-5eb0-9f6f-bcc3b133cfd6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163222 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/0f/0f62a89f8da1ab7bfb86d46fddb441e95e5e3a9554e5a88662691b4df40e19af.svg)\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n![](images/78/780de3c3f66b1037e4152224f4aa98b65b8b1b859adfe7c3881bb830d7eecd66.svg)\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161771 :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](images/fb/fb428c39bbc03be798bca2463658676de5c684396001cf547e9395352f456bc9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](images/3f/3f91ec40e717eac93bb38f9c13f7b1700e84ac109ccceaffbb8473ba8d6b4e34.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163254 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n![](images/e4/e41d28b6c6562ada530024fe6ab29cc16447dce14b2f741b8b35758618c78103.svg)\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." :tutorial/title "Properties of Symmetric Matrices"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161816 :question/id #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8478" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/81/812e9888b920e0338fd72116464b8b96c8c272f95f180cd46aa3f1db9afac8d2.svg)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}}] :activity/title "Symmetric Matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162214}]}
